    "abstract": "Abstract Greenhouse agriculture is increasingly vital for food security. Yet, its catchment-scale hydrological effects remain poorly understood. We develop an integrated framework combining greenhouse climate modelling and remote sensing of land cover with the physically based CATchment HYdrology (CATHY) surface\u2013subsurface hydrological model. We apply it to the Piana del Sele district in southern Italy to assess trade-offs under land use and climate change scenarios. Our simulations show that, compared to open fields, greenhouses theoretically reduce evapotranspiration; however, their effects on irrigation requirements depend strongly on the timing of crop cycles relative to rainfall. Moreover, greenhouses increase streamflow and reduce groundwater storage. Climate change intensifies summer irrigation demand, although halting greenhouse cultivation in summer mitigates this pressure. These results demonstrate how greenhouse expansion reshapes regional water balances and highlight the need for integrated modelling to inform water management and provide a transferable basis for policies balancing food production and water security.",
    "field": "Agricultural hydrology",
    "problem": "The catchment-scale hydrological effects of expanding greenhouse agriculture are poorly understood, hindering the ability to model and manage the resulting trade-offs between food production and regional water security under changing land use and climate conditions.",
    "method": "The paper develops an integrated framework combining greenhouse climate modelling and remote sensing of land cover with a physically-based surface-subsurface hydrological model to assess the catchment-scale hydrological effects of greenhouse expansion.",
    "task": "The task is to assess the hydrological effects and water-balance trade-offs of greenhouse expansion in a Mediterranean catchment by developing and applying an integrated framework that combines greenhouse climate modelling, remote sensing, and a surface-subsurface hydrological model under various land use and climate change scenarios.",
